{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc \"git diff dec2e64c..9c3819d -- POSE.md locales/pt-BR/POSE.md pose-mcp/internal/cli/help_catalog.go; sed -n '1,160p' pose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_carry_test.go; sed -n '1710,1805p' pose-mcp/internal/cli/validate.go; cat pose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_engine_identity_test.go\"","aggregated_output":"diff --git a/POSE.md b/POSE.md\nindex 4621ed21..3f2055de 100644\n--- a/POSE.md\n+++ b/POSE.md\n@@ -215,7 +215,7 @@ pose new-knowledge <type> <slug>   # create handoff/note/decision-log\n pose check [--strict|--tolerant]   # structural integrity + matrix schema +\n                                    # task-map sync + spec graph + schema version\n pose validate [--strict|--tolerant] [--stack s] [--module path] [--report] [--json-out f] [--junit f] [--sarif f]\n-              [--changed-from rev [--changed-to rev]] [--jobs n] [--explain] [--emit-plan f]\n+              [--changed-from rev [--changed-to rev] | --carry] [--jobs n] [--explain] [--emit-plan f]\n pose lint-spec <slug>|--all [--strict|--tolerant] [--required-only] [--ready-check] [--design-check]\n pose knowledge-check [--strict|--tolerant] [--max-overdue N]\n pose recurrence-check [--strict|--tolerant] [--window-days N] [--threshold T] [--flap-threshold N] [--include-pass]\n@@ -350,7 +350,9 @@ pose release-package-manifests --version X.Y.Z --checksums <checksums.txt> --out\n - `new-knowledge` — creates an artifact in [`.pose/knowledge/`](.pose/knowledge/) from [`.pose/templates/knowledge.md`](.pose/templates/knowledge.md) with mandatory frontmatter (`type`, `owner`, `sensitivity`, `created_at`, `last_reviewed_at`, `expires_at`). Computes `expires_at` from the TTL (default 30d, max 90d).\n - `validate` — runs the declarative matrix in [`validation-matrix.json`](.pose/indexes/validation-matrix.json): per-stack checks, per-module overrides, severity (`required`/`optional`) and mode (`strict`/`tolerant`). `--json-out`/`--junit`/`--sarif <path>` (`--json <path>` still works as a deprecated alias of `--json-out <path>`) emit the versioned structured result (schema 1) from one canonical model: stable check IDs (`<module>/<stack>/<name>`), command metadata, timing, severity, distinguishable outcomes (`pass|fail|error|skipped` — infra failures never masquerade as check failures), deterministic skip reasons, bounded captured output and secret redaction (configured env values only; inherited environment never enters the result). Text output stays authoritative; machine formats are additive. POSE-specific semantics survive the JUnit/SARIF projections via documented extensions (classname suffix / `pose/*` properties).\n   **Runtime guardrails:** every check runs under a timeout (`timeoutSeconds` per check, `defaults.timeoutSeconds`, safe default 600s) and an output ceiling (`defaults.maxOutputBytes`, default 1 MiB); breaching either terminates the process group and records the explicit state (`limit_state: timeout|output-limit`). Checks marked `isolation: \"required\"` never run locally — they are skipped with a machine-readable reason and exported by `--emit-plan <file>`: an execution-plan envelope binding project, spec, check plan, matrix digest, git HEAD and an approval slot to be stamped with an expiring execution identity before the Harness may run it.\n-  **Changed scope:** `--changed-from <rev> [--changed-to <rev>]` selects the minimum safe module set deterministically — modules containing changed files (tracked and untracked), transitive dependents via `dependsOn` edges in [`module-metadata.json`](.pose/indexes/module-metadata.json), and policy widening (criticality `high` always runs). A change outside every module runs everything (uncertainty prefers safe execution); unselected checks are recorded as skipped with the selection reason and `--explain` prints every decision. Revisions are confined to a safe grammar; without the flags, full validation is unchanged.\n+  **Changed scope:** `--changed-from <rev> [--changed-to <rev>]` selects the minimum safe module set deterministically — modules containing changed files (tracked and untracked), transitive dependents via `dependsOn` edges in [`module-metadata.json`](.pose/indexes/module-metadata.json), and policy widening (criticality `high` always runs). Changed names are read NUL-delimited and a rename names both its source and its destination. Each changed file selects its most specific owning module, every module enclosing that owner (the root module encloses them all), every module beneath it (a gitlink or replaced directory changes what lies under it), and every module whose declared `dependsOn` contains it, lies beneath it or is the root `.`, even after that dependency is deleted. A file no module owns selects the root module. Module metadata that does not decode stops the selection (a full run under `--carry`, an error under `--changed-from`). A change outside every module runs every module that does not declare `\"selfContained\": true` in its metadata (uncertainty prefers safe execution; the declaration states that the module's checks read only its own directory and its `dependsOn` modules); unselected checks are recorded as skipped with the selection reason and `--explain` prints every decision. Revisions are confined to a safe grammar; without the flags, full validation is unchanged.\n+\n+  **Carried checks:** `--carry --json-out <results_path>` refreshes the canonical result instead of rerunning the whole matrix. It uses the current canonical result as its base when the base is comparable: same schema, mode, matrix digest and `engine_version` (the engine version plus the VCS revision the binary was built from; a build of a modified tree never carries), unfiltered, not cancelled (a signal cancels the whole run, even an all-carried one), recorded `material_clean` (it validated an unchanged working copy of its own head: no tracked or untracked unignored material changed and HEAD did not move while it ran, as its reflog and every submodule's reflog show; mode changes count whatever `core.fileMode` says, and every submodule must hold its recorded commit with no nested submodule, checked both before the plan is read and when the run ends; a spec whose material the engine cannot compute stays unavailable in any clean run and does not refuse the base), with its `git_head` an ancestor of `HEAD`, a clean material working copy now, a case-sensitive filesystem with no case-folded directory on the path to a module or a dependency, no module reached through a symlink or sharing its directory with another module, no tracked symlink whose path or target lies in a module other than the root or in a declared `dependsOn` (or whose target leaves the repository), every submodule's tree included (a submodule that is not checked out or has nested submodules refuses), no declared dependency outside the repository or written as an unclean path or resolving through a symlink (tracked or ignored), only ASCII module and dependency paths, no index entry marked assume-unchanged or skip-worktree, and no check id defined twice, and working module metadata equal to HEAD's. Changed names are read with every gitlink shown, whatever the Git configuration. The head is pinned before planning, and a run whose HEAD moved records its material bindings as unavailable. It runs the modules the changed-scope rules select between the base head and `HEAD`; the canonical result file and the derived administrative records that material bindings ignore (data files that are still regular files in the derived-record directories under `.pose/`: indexes, results, state, assessments and review records; the validation matrix stays material) are not changes. A module whose checks are not all in the base also runs. Every other check keeps the base result, its outcome included, and records `carried_from` (the head it ran at, kept through chained carries) and `carried_generated_at`; the delivery index reads that head for the result, so evidence freshness judges the commit a carried check really observed. A carried failure still fails the run. Any unmet condition runs everything, and `carry.reason` says why. `carry` records the base, the number of changed files and each selected module's reason, and `counts.carried` the reused checks. `--carry` does not combine with `--module`, `--stack`, `--workspace`, `--root-only`, `--changed-from` or `--changed-to`.\n \n   **Concurrency:** checks run up to `--jobs <n>` at a time (default: half the cores, bounded by available memory at about 1.5 GiB per check and by 8); results are reported and recorded in matrix order, whatever order they finish in, and the report records `jobs` and `wall_seconds`. `--jobs 1` and `--verbose` run one at a time. A check marked `exclusive: true` runs with nothing else running: earlier checks finish first, so its `when` can depend on them, which a concurrent check's cannot — a check's `when` is read as it starts. The run ends with its wall time beside the sum of its checks and the slowest checks. An interrupt or SIGTERM stops every running check's process group (on Windows, its process) and starts nothing more; those checks are recorded with `limit_state: cancelled`, the run is `fail` and exits 1, and a second signal acts at once. A default `--jobs` follows the cgroup's memory headroom and CPU quota where there is one.\n \ndiff --git a/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md b/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md\nindex df424ce4..3c20d024 100644\n--- a/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md\n+++ b/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md\n@@ -219,7 +219,7 @@ pose new-knowledge <type> <slug>   # cria handoff/note/decision-log em .pose/kno\n pose check [--strict|--tolerant]   # integridade estrutural + matrix schema +\n                                    # task-map sync + grafo de specs + schema version\n pose validate [--strict|--tolerant] [--stack s] [--module path] [--report] [--json-out f] [--junit f] [--sarif f]\n-              [--changed-from rev [--changed-to rev]] [--jobs n] [--explain] [--emit-plan f]\n+              [--changed-from rev [--changed-to rev] | --carry] [--jobs n] [--explain] [--emit-plan f]\n pose lint-spec <slug>|--all [--strict|--tolerant] [--required-only] [--ready-check] [--design-check]\n pose knowledge-check [--strict|--tolerant] [--max-overdue N]\n pose recurrence-check [--strict|--tolerant] [--window-days N] [--threshold T] [--flap-threshold N] [--include-pass]\n@@ -354,7 +354,9 @@ pose release-package-manifests --version X.Y.Z --checksums <checksums.txt> --out\n - `new-knowledge` — cria artefato em [`.pose/knowledge/`](.pose/knowledge/) a partir de [`.pose/templates/knowledge.md`](.pose/templates/knowledge.md) com frontmatter obrigatório (`type`, `owner`, `sensitivity`, `created_at`, `last_reviewed_at`, `expires_at`). Calcula `expires_at` pelo TTL (default 30d, máximo 90d).\n - `validate` — executa a matriz declarativa em [`validation-matrix.json`](.pose/indexes/validation-matrix.json): checks por stack, overrides por módulo, severidade (`required`/`optional`) e modo (`strict`/`tolerant`). `--json-out`/`--junit`/`--sarif <path>` (`--json <path>` continua funcionando como alias depreciado de `--json-out <path>`) emitem o resultado estruturado versionado (schema 1) a partir de um único modelo canônico: IDs estáveis de check (`<module>/<stack>/<name>`), metadados de comando, tempo, severidade, outcomes distinguíveis (`pass|fail|error|skipped` — falha de infraestrutura nunca se disfarça de falha de check), motivos determinísticos de skip, saída capturada com limite e redação de segredos (apenas valores de env configurados; o ambiente herdado nunca entra no resultado). A saída em texto continua autoritativa; os formatos de máquina são aditivos. A semântica específica do POSE sobrevive às projeções JUnit/SARIF via extensões documentadas (sufixo de classname / propriedades `pose/*`).\n   **Guardrails de runtime:** todo check roda sob timeout (`timeoutSeconds` por check, `defaults.timeoutSeconds`, default seguro 600s) e teto de saída (`defaults.maxOutputBytes`, default 1 MiB); violar qualquer um encerra o process group e registra o estado explícito (`limit_state: timeout|output-limit`). Checks marcados `isolation: \"required\"` nunca rodam localmente — são pulados com motivo legível por máquina e exportados por `--emit-plan <file>`: um envelope de plano de execução vinculando projeto, spec, plano de checks, digest da matriz, git HEAD e um slot de aprovação a ser carimbado com identidade de execução expirável antes que o Harness possa executá-lo.\n-  **Escopo por mudança:** `--changed-from <rev> [--changed-to <rev>]` seleciona deterministicamente o conjunto mínimo seguro de módulos — módulos com arquivos alterados (rastreados e não rastreados), dependentes transitivos via arestas `dependsOn` em [`module-metadata.json`](.pose/indexes/module-metadata.json) e alargamento por política (criticidade `high` sempre roda). Uma mudança fora de todos os módulos roda tudo (na incerteza, prefere-se execução segura); checks não selecionados são registrados como skipped com o motivo da seleção e `--explain` imprime cada decisão. Revisões ficam confinadas a uma gramática segura; sem as flags, a validação completa é inalterada.\n+  **Escopo por mudança:** `--changed-from <rev> [--changed-to <rev>]` seleciona deterministicamente o conjunto mínimo seguro de módulos — módulos com arquivos alterados (rastreados e não rastreados), dependentes transitivos via arestas `dependsOn` em [`module-metadata.json`](.pose/indexes/module-metadata.json) e alargamento por política (criticidade `high` sempre roda). Os nomes alterados são lidos delimitados por NUL, e um rename nomeia a origem e o destino. Cada arquivo alterado seleciona o módulo dono mais específico, todo módulo que envolve esse dono (o módulo raiz envolve todos), todo módulo abaixo dele (um gitlink ou diretório substituído altera o que está abaixo) e todo módulo cujo `dependsOn` declarado o contém, está abaixo dele ou é a raiz `.`, mesmo depois que essa dependência é apagada. Um arquivo que nenhum módulo possui seleciona o módulo raiz. Metadados de módulo que não decodificam interrompem a seleção (execução completa com `--carry`, erro com `--changed-from`). Uma mudança fora de todos os módulos roda todo módulo que não declara `\"selfContained\": true` nos seus metadados (na incerteza, prefere-se execução segura; a declaração afirma que os checks do módulo só leem o próprio diretório e os módulos de `dependsOn`); checks não selecionados são registrados como skipped com o motivo da seleção e `--explain` imprime cada decisão. Revisões ficam confinadas a uma gramática segura; sem as flags, a validação completa é inalterada.\n+\n+  **Checks carregados:** `--carry --json-out <results_path>` renova o resultado canônico em vez de rodar a matriz inteira de novo. Ele usa o resultado canônico atual como base quando a base é comparável: mesmo schema, modo, digest da matriz e `engine_version` (a versão do motor mais a revisão VCS da qual o binário foi compilado; um build de árvore modificada nunca carrega), sem filtro, não cancelada (um sinal cancela o run inteiro, mesmo um só com checks carregados), com `material_clean` registrado (validou uma cópia de trabalho inalterada do próprio head: nenhum material rastreado ou não rastreado e não ignorado mudou, e o HEAD não se moveu durante o run, segundo o reflog dele e de todo submódulo; mudanças de modo contam qualquer que seja `core.fileMode`, e todo submódulo precisa estar no commit registrado, sem submódulo aninhado, verificado antes da leitura do plano e no fim do run; uma spec cujo material o motor não consegue calcular continua indisponível em qualquer run limpo e não recusa a base), com `git_head` ancestral de `HEAD`, cópia de trabalho limpa agora nos paths materiais sistema de arquivos que diferencia maiúsculas, sem diretório com casefold no caminho até um módulo ou uma dependência, nenhum módulo alcançado por symlink ou compartilhando diretório com outro módulo, nenhum symlink rastreado cujo path ou alvo esteja num módulo que não seja a raiz ou num `dependsOn` declarado (ou cujo alvo saia do repositório), inclusive na árvore de todo submódulo (um submódulo sem checkout ou com submódulos aninhados recusa), nenhuma dependência declarada fora do repositório ou escrita como path não limpo ou que resolve por symlink (rastreado ou ignorado), apenas paths ASCII de módulo e dependência, nenhuma entrada de índice marcada assume-unchanged ou skip-worktree, e nenhum id de check definido duas vezes e metadados de módulo da cópia de trabalho iguais aos do HEAD. Os nomes alterados são lidos com todo gitlink visível, qualquer que seja a configuração do Git. O head é fixado antes do planejamento, e um run cujo HEAD se moveu registra seus bindings de material como indisponíveis. Roda os módulos que as regras de escopo por mudança selecionam entre o head da base e `HEAD`; o arquivo do resultado canônico e os registros administrativos derivados que o binding de material ignora (arquivos de dados ainda regulares nos diretórios de registros derivados sob `.pose/`: índices, resultados, estado, assessments e registros de revisão; a matriz de validação continua material) não contam como mudança. Um módulo cujos checks não estão todos na base também roda. Todo outro check mantém o resultado da base, inclusive o desfecho, e registra `carried_from` (o head em que rodou, mantido em carregamentos encadeados) e `carried_generated_at`; o índice de entrega lê esse head no resultado, então o frescor da evidência julga o commit que um check carregado realmente observou. Uma falha carregada continua reprovando o run. Qualquer condição não atendida roda tudo, e `carry.reason` diz por quê. `carry` registra a base, o número de arquivos alterados e o motivo de cada módulo selecionado, e `counts.carried` os checks reaproveitados. `--carry` não combina com `--module`, `--stack`, `--workspace`, `--root-only`, `--changed-from` ou `--changed-to`.\n \n   **Concorrência:** os checks rodam até `--jobs <n>` ao mesmo tempo (padrão: metade dos núcleos, limitada pela memória disponível, cerca de 1,5 GiB por check, e por 8); os resultados são mostrados e registrados na ordem da matriz, seja qual for a ordem em que terminem, e o relatório registra `jobs` e `wall_seconds`. `--jobs 1` e `--verbose` rodam um de cada vez. Um check marcado `exclusive: true` roda sem nenhum outro em execução: os checks anteriores terminam antes, então seu `when` pode depender deles, o que o de um check concorrente não pode — o `when` de um check é lido quando ele vai começar. A execução termina com seu tempo de parede ao lado da soma dos checks e os checks mais lentos. Uma interrupção ou SIGTERM encerra o grupo de processos de cada check em execução (no Windows, seu processo) e não inicia mais nada; esses checks são registrados com `limit_state: cancelled`, a execução fica `fail` e sai com 1, e um segundo sinal age na hora. O `--jobs` padrão segue a folga de memória e a cota de CPU do cgroup, quando há um.\n \ndiff --git a/pose-mcp/internal/cli/help_catalog.go b/pose-mcp/internal/cli/help_catalog.go\nindex f252f70a..c6381d05 100644\n--- a/pose-mcp/internal/cli/help_catalog.go\n+++ b/pose-mcp/internal/cli/help_catalog.go\n@@ -129,6 +129,7 @@ var commandHelpCatalog = map[string]CommandHelp{\n \t\t\t{\"--json-out <path>\", \"Write structured validation outcome to the specified JSON path\", \"Grava o resultado da validação no caminho JSON especificado\"},\n \t\t\t{\"--json <path>\", \"Deprecated alias of --json-out <path>\", \"Alias depreciado de --json-out <path>\"},\n \t\t\t{\"--changed-from <rev>\", \"Validate only modules affected between git revisions\", \"Valida apenas módulos afetados entre as revisões git\"},\n+\t\t\t{\"--carry\", \"Refresh the canonical result: run what changed since it and carry the other checks with the head they ran at\", \"Renova o resultado canônico: roda o que mudou desde ele e carrega os demais checks com o head em que rodaram\"},\n \t\t\t{\"--jobs <n>\", \"Run up to n checks at once (default: from cores and available memory)\", \"Roda até n checks ao mesmo tempo (padrão: conforme núcleos e memória disponível)\"},\n \t\t\t{\"--verbose\", \"Stream each check's output as it runs; by default it is captured and a failing check's tail is shown\", \"Transmite a saída de cada check durante a execução; por padrão ela é capturada e o final da saída de um check que falha é exibido\"},\n \t\t},\npackage cli\n\n// Carried checks (spec pose-validate-carries-unchanged-checks): validate\n// --carry refreshes the canonical result by running only what a change can\n// affect and carrying the rest with the head it really ran at.\n\nimport (\n\t\"crypto/sha256\"\n\t\"encoding/hex\"\n\t\"encoding/json\"\n\t\"os\"\n\t\"os/exec\"\n\t\"path/filepath\"\n\t\"sort\"\n\t\"strings\"\n\t\"testing\"\n\n\tposemodel \"github.com/harne8/pose-mcp/internal/pose\"\n)\n\nconst carryResults = \".pose/results/delivery-validation.json\"\n\ntype carryFixture struct {\n\tt       *testing.T\n\troot    string\n\tcounter string\n}\n\n// newCarryFixture builds a committed repository with Go modules a and b and\n// a root file. Every check appends its module to a counter file outside the\n// tree, so a test can tell an executed check from a carried one.\nfunc newCarryFixture(t *testing.T, metadata string) *carryFixture {\n\tt.Helper()\n\tf := &carryFixture{t: t, root: t.TempDir(), counter: filepath.Join(t.TempDir(), \"runs\")}\n\tf.write(\"a/go.mod\", \"module a\\n\")\n\tf.write(\"b/go.mod\", \"module b\\n\")\n\tf.write(\"root.txt\", \"root\\n\")\n\tf.write(\".gitignore\", \".pose/transfers/.authority-transfer.lock\\n\")\n\tf.write(\".pose/policy/delivery.json\", `{\"schema_version\":1,\"enabled\":true,\"results_path\":\"`+carryResults+`\"}`)\n\tmatrix := `{\"defaults\":{\"mode\":\"strict\"},\"stacks\":{\"go\":{\"checks\":[` +\n\t\t`{\"name\":\"ok\",\"program\":\"sh\",\"args\":[\"-c\",\"basename \\\"$PWD\\\" >> ` + f.counter + `; test ! -f fail\"],\"severity\":\"required\",\"evidenceClass\":\"unit\"}]}}}`\n\tf.write(\".pose/indexes/validation-matrix.json\", matrix)\n\tif metadata != \"\" {\n\t\tf.write(\".pose/indexes/module-metadata.json\", metadata)\n\t}\n\tf.git(\"init\", \"-q\")\n\tf.commit(\"base\")\n\treturn f\n}\n\nfunc (f *carryFixture) write(rel, content string) {\n\tf.t.Helper()\n\tpath := filepath.Join(f.root, rel)\n\tif err := os.MkdirAll(filepath.Dir(path), 0o755); err != nil {\n\t\tf.t.Fatal(err)\n\t}\n\tif err := os.WriteFile(path, []byte(content), 0o644); err != nil {\n\t\tf.t.Fatal(err)\n\t}\n}\n\nfunc (f *carryFixture) git(args ...string) string {\n\tf.t.Helper()\n\tcmd := exec.Command(\"git\", append([]string{\"-C\", f.root}, args...)...)\n\tcmd.Env = append(os.Environ(), \"GIT_AUTHOR_NAME=t\", \"GIT_AUTHOR_EMAIL=t@t\", \"GIT_COMMITTER_NAME=t\", \"GIT_COMMITTER_EMAIL=t@t\", \"GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL=/dev/null\")\n\tout, err := cmd.CombinedOutput()\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\tf.t.Fatalf(\"git %v: %v %s\", args, err, out)\n\t}\n\treturn strings.TrimSpace(string(out))\n}\n\nfunc (f *carryFixture) commit(message string) string {\n\tf.t.Helper()\n\tf.git(\"add\", \"-A\")\n\tf.git(\"-c\", \"core.hooksPath=/dev/null\", \"commit\", \"-qm\", message, \"--allow-empty\")\n\treturn f.git(\"rev-parse\", \"HEAD\")\n}\n\n// validate runs pose validate and returns its exit code, output and the\n// canonical record it wrote. The counter is reset first.\nfunc (f *carryFixture) validate(args ...string) (int, string, validationRunResult) {\n\tf.t.Helper()\n\t_ = os.Remove(f.counter)\n\tcode, out := runValidate(f.t, f.root, append(args, \"--json-out\", carryResults)...)\n\tvar run validationRunResult\n\tif raw, err := os.ReadFile(filepath.Join(f.root, carryResults)); err == nil {\n\t\tif err := json.Unmarshal(raw, &run); err != nil {\n\t\t\tf.t.Fatal(err)\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn code, out, run\n}\n\nfunc (f *carryFixture) ran() []string {\n\traw, err := os.ReadFile(f.counter)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn nil\n\t}\n\truns := strings.Fields(string(raw))\n\tsort.Strings(runs)\n\treturn runs\n}\n\nfunc checkOf(run validationRunResult, module string) checkResult {\n\tfor _, c := range run.Checks {\n\t\tif c.Module == module {\n\t\t\treturn c\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn checkResult{}\n}\n\n// baseline runs the full matrix, commits the canonical record and returns the\n// head the record validated.\nfunc (f *carryFixture) baseline() string {\n\tf.t.Helper()\n\tvalidated := f.git(\"rev-parse\", \"HEAD\")\n\tcode, out, _ := f.validate()\n\tif code != 0 {\n\t\tf.t.Fatalf(\"baseline exit=%d %s\", code, out)\n\t}\n\tf.commit(\"record\")\n\treturn validated\n}\n\nfunc TestCarryRunsOnlyTheChangedModule(t *testing.T) {\n\tf := newCarryFixture(t, \"\")\n\tbase := f.baseline()\n\tf.write(\"a/changed.go\", \"package a\\n\")\n\thead := f.commit(\"change a\")\n\tcode, out, run := f.validate(\"--carry\")\n\tif code != 0 {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"exit=%d %s\", code, out)\n\t}\n\tif got := strings.Join(f.ran(), \",\"); got != \"a\" {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"executed modules = %q, want only a\", got)\n\t}\n\tif run.Carry == nil || !run.Carry.Used || run.Carry.BaseHead != base {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"carry = %+v, want used from %s\", run.Carry, base)\n\t}\n\tif b := checkOf(run, \"b\"); b.Outcome != \"pass\" || b.CarriedFrom != base || b.CarriedGeneratedAt == \"\" {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"b = %+v, want a pass carried from %s\", b, base)\n\t}\n\tif a := checkOf(run, \"a\"); a.CarriedFrom != \"\" || a.Outcome != \"pass\" {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"a = %+v, want executed\", a)\n\t}\n\tif run.GitHead != head || run.Counts.Carried != 1 || run.Counts.Executed != 1 || run.Counts.Passed != 2 {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"head=%s counts=%+v\", run.GitHead, run.Counts)\n\t}\n\tif run.EngineVersion == \"\" {\n\t\tt.Fatal(\"engine_version must be recorded\")\n\t}\n}\n\nfunc TestCarryKeepsTheOriginalHeadAcrossChainedCarries(t *testing.T) {\n\tf := newCarryFixture(t, \"\")\n\tbase := f.baseline()\n\tf.write(\"a/one.go\", \"package a\\n\")\n\tf.commit(\"change a\")\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn newArgs\n}\n\n// materialWorktreeClean reports whether the working copy equals HEAD in every\n// path outside the administrative classes, untracked files included. A status\n// that cannot be read is not clean.\nfunc materialWorktreeClean(root string) bool {\n\t// Mode changes count whatever core.fileMode says (review round 13, N36).\n\tout, err := gitOutputBounded(root, 16*1024*1024, \"-c\", \"core.fileMode=true\", \"status\", \"--porcelain=v1\", \"-z\", \"--ignore-submodules=none\", \"--untracked-files=all\")\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn false\n\t}\n\trecords := strings.Split(string(out), \"\\x00\")\n\tvar administrative []string\n\tfor i := 0; i < len(records); i++ {\n\t\trecord := records[i]\n\t\tif len(record) < 4 {\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}\n\t\tpaths := []string{record[3:]}\n\t\tif record[0] == 'R' || record[0] == 'C' || record[1] == 'R' || record[1] == 'C' {\n\t\t\t// A rename or copy carries its source as the next record.\n\t\t\tif i+1 < len(records) {\n\t\t\t\ti++\n\t\t\t\tpaths = append(paths, records[i])\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t\tfor _, p := range paths {\n\t\t\tif !posemodel.IsAdministrativePath(p) {\n\t\t\t\treturn false\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tadministrative = append(administrative, p)\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn administrativeRecordsAreRegular(root, administrative)\n}\n\n// materialTrackedUnchanged reports whether no tracked file differs from HEAD\n// outside the administrative records. Untracked files are not counted: checks\n// leave build products behind, and those are not the material they ran over.\nfunc materialTrackedUnchanged(root string) bool {\n\tout, err := gitOutputBounded(root, 16*1024*1024, \"-c\", \"core.fileMode=true\", \"status\", \"--porcelain=v1\", \"-z\", \"--ignore-submodules=none\", \"--untracked-files=no\")\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn false\n\t}\n\trecords := strings.Split(string(out), \"\\x00\")\n\tvar administrative []string\n\tfor i := 0; i < len(records); i++ {\n\t\trecord := records[i]\n\t\tif len(record) < 4 {\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}\n\t\tpaths := []string{record[3:]}\n\t\tif record[0] == 'R' || record[0] == 'C' || record[1] == 'R' || record[1] == 'C' {\n\t\t\tif i+1 < len(records) {\n\t\t\t\ti++\n\t\t\t\tpaths = append(paths, records[i])\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t\tfor _, p := range paths {\n\t\t\tif !posemodel.IsAdministrativePath(p) {\n\t\t\t\treturn false\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tadministrative = append(administrative, p)\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn administrativeRecordsAreRegular(root, administrative)\n}\n\n// administrativeRecordsAreRegular reports whether every changed administrative\n// path is a regular file at HEAD (or new): editing a committed symlink or\n// submodule inside a class is a material change, as in MaterialSetDigest.\nfunc administrativeRecordsAreRegular(root string, paths []string) bool {\n\tif len(paths) == 0 {\n\t\treturn true\n\t}\n\targs := []string{\"ls-tree\", \"-z\", \"HEAD\", \"--\"}\n\tfor _, p := range paths {\n\t\tif strings.ContainsAny(p, \"\\n\\x00\") {\n\t\t\treturn false\n\t\t}\n\t\t// The working copy as well as HEAD: a regular record replaced by a link, or\n\t\t// a new link, is not a record.\n\t\tif info, err := os.Lstat(filepath.Join(root, filepath.FromSlash(p))); err == nil && !info.Mode().IsRegular() {\n\t\t\treturn false\n\t\t}\n\t\targs = append(args, \":(literal)\"+p)\n\t}\n\tout, err := gitOutputBounded(root, 8*1024*1024, args...)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn false\n\t}\n\tfor _, record := range strings.Split(string(out), \"\\x00\") {\n\t\tmeta, _, ok := strings.Cut(record, \"\\t\")\npackage cli\n\nimport (\n\t\"runtime/debug\"\n\t\"testing\"\n\n\t\"github.com/harne8/pose-mcp/internal/version\"\n)\n\n// A modified build is not a comparable engine identity (spec\n// pose-validate-carries-unchanged-checks, review round 2, N4).\nfunc TestEngineIdentityRefusesModifiedBuilds(t *testing.T) {\n\tinfo := func(settings ...debug.BuildSetting) *debug.BuildInfo { return &debug.BuildInfo{Settings: settings} }\n\tcases := []struct {\n\t\tname       string\n\t\tinfo       *debug.BuildInfo\n\t\twant       string\n\t\tverifiable bool\n\t}{\n\t\t{\"clean\", info(debug.BuildSetting{Key: \"vcs.revision\", Value: \"abc\"}, debug.BuildSetting{Key: \"vcs.modified\", Value: \"false\"}), version.Version + \"+abc\", true},\n\t\t{\"modified\", info(debug.BuildSetting{Key: \"vcs.revision\", Value: \"abc\"}, debug.BuildSetting{Key: \"vcs.modified\", Value: \"true\"}), version.Version + \"+abc.dirty\", false},\n\t\t{\"unstamped\", info(), version.Version, true},\n\t}\n\tfor _, tc := range cases {\n\t\tgot, verifiable := engineIdentityFrom(tc.info, true)\n\t\tif got != tc.want || verifiable != tc.verifiable {\n\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"%s: identity %q verifiable %v, want %q %v\", tc.name, got, verifiable, tc.want, tc.verifiable)\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
